WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.660
Welcome to This Explainer. Okay, let's
jump right in, shall we? Today we're

00:03.660 --> 00:07.400
unpacking a really fascinating paradoxing
business. How is it that perfectly

00:07.400 --> 00:11.860
designed metrics can sometimes absolutely
destroy a perfectly good product? To

00:11.860 --> 00:14.820
figure this out, we're going to dive into
the story of a small, beloved Sichuan

00:14.820 --> 00:18.200
restaurant called Deng Ying Lau. It's
actually a brilliant case study from

00:18.200 --> 00:21.260
recent management control curriculums. It
teaches us pretty much everything we need

00:21.260 --> 00:24.720
to know about key performance indicators,
strategic control, and what it really,

00:25.020 --> 00:28.880
truly means to be a purpose-driven
organization. At the very heart of Deng

00:28.880 --> 00:33.380
Yun Lu was its owner and head chef, Aunt
Lin. She made this incredible signature

00:33.380 --> 00:37.640
traditional boiled beef. And, you know,
Aunt Lin had this beautiful way of

00:37.640 --> 00:41.800
describing her cooking. She always said,
the first spoon gives color, the second

00:41.800 --> 00:46.380
gives aroma, but the third spoon of red
oil gives the dish its soul. She

00:46.380 --> 00:50.100
completely understood that true quality
wasn't just following a recipe on a piece

00:50.100 --> 00:54.780
of paper, right? It took perfect timing,
deep intuition, and this unquantifiable

00:54.780 --> 00:59.500
human touch. That crucial third spoon of
hot oil. If you pour it a second too

00:59.500 --> 01:02.740
early, the aroma just doesn't rise. A
second too late, and the chili turns

01:02.740 --> 01:09.880
bitter. Section one, the perfect metric
trap when data replaces intuition. So as

01:09.880 --> 01:13.260
the neighborhood turned into this bustling
tourist spot and rent went through the

01:13.260 --> 01:19.280
roof, Deng Ying Lu really had to adapt.
Enter a new manager named Zo Ming. Now, Zo

01:19.280 --> 01:23.260
Ming was highly capable, and after taking
a hard look at the books, he decided the

01:23.260 --> 01:27.780
restaurant just couldn't rely solely on a
chef's traditional intuition anymore. He

01:27.780 --> 01:31.940
wanted clear, data-driven management.
Basically, he wanted control. And

01:31.940 --> 01:36.380
honestly, Zo Ming's approach made a pun of
logical sense at first. He rolled out this

01:36.380 --> 01:40.460
incredibly rigid KPI grading sheet for
every single dish on the menu. Every week,

01:40.620 --> 01:43.760
front of house and kitchen staff would sit
down and review these numbers. If your

01:43.760 --> 01:47.140
numbers were green, boom, you got a
reward. If your numbers were red, well,

01:47.300 --> 01:51.140
you had some explaining to do. And
initially, it works like absolute magic.

01:51.340 --> 01:55.280
The kitchen sped up, food waste dropped,
margins went up, and the tourists were

01:55.280 --> 02:00.420
leaving great reviews. It looks like a
total undeniable success. Section two,

02:00.820 --> 02:06.460
decisions dictate the data. The behavioral
backfire. Because, well, as the numbers on

02:06.460 --> 02:10.800
Zo Ming's spreadsheet got greener and
greener, a completely silent crisis was

02:10.800 --> 02:14.900
happening in the kitchen. The food was
fundamentally losing its soul. The

02:14.900 --> 02:17.720
restaurant's hardcore regulars started
noticing the dishes lacked their usual

02:17.720 --> 02:21.880
punch, and the kitchen itself went
unnervingly quiet. The chefs weren't

02:21.880 --> 02:25.100
listening to the sound of the walk anymore
or smelling the spices to check if they

02:25.100 --> 02:28.400
were ready. They were literally just
staring at the clock to hit their metrics.

02:29.060 --> 02:34.100
This perfectly illustrates the whole
concept of gaming the system. To hit those

02:34.100 --> 02:38.340
strict new speed targets, chefs started
pre-cooking ingredients so they were

02:38.340 --> 02:42.620
already half done. To hit the lower cost
targets, they just sliced the beef

02:42.620 --> 02:46.780
thinner. And to minimize dishes being
returned by tourists who couldn't handle

02:46.780 --> 02:52.440
real Sichuan heat, they watered down that
authentic, fiery flavor. They did exactly

02:52.440 --> 02:56.700
what the spreadsheet incentivized them to
do. And in the process, they totally

02:56.700 --> 03:01.880
sacrificed the actual essence of the food.
This whole restaurant saga isn't just a

03:01.880 --> 03:06.260
cooking lesson. It maps perfectly onto a
five-step knowledge chain for designing

03:06.260 --> 03:10.240
strategic management controls. Whether
you're running a local restaurant or a

03:10.240 --> 03:14.780
global tech giant, the steps are the same.
You have to define the decision, pick

03:14.780 --> 03:18.120
information that's actually useful,
predict how people are going to try and

03:18.120 --> 03:22.020
game your metrics, match your control
style to the level of uncertainty, and,

03:22.160 --> 03:26.640
finally, let your core purpose act as a
gatekeeper for your resources. Moving to

03:26.640 --> 03:32.480
Section 3, Predicting Behavioral Backfire,
Measuring the Wrong Things. Step 3 of our

03:32.480 --> 03:37.380
framework comes with a massive warning for
anyone in management. The absolute second

03:37.380 --> 03:42.020
you tie a metric to rewards, promotions,
or rankings, it instantly stops being just

03:42.020 --> 03:46.080
a measurement. It becomes a strict
behavioral rule. People are going to

03:46.080 --> 03:49.580
optimize for that metric, even if it
actively hurts the overall goal.

03:50.300 --> 03:54.100
Management accountants actually have a
term for what Zhou Ming fell victim to.

03:54.280 --> 03:59.240
It's called false precision. It's
basically when you use exact, easily

03:59.240 --> 04:04.660
trackable numbers to mask highly uncertain
inputs or qualitative judgments. Zhou

04:04.660 --> 04:08.220
Ming's pristine spreadsheet totally
blinded him to the declining quality of

04:08.220 --> 04:12.380
the food. Because he couldn't neatly
measure soul or authenticity, he just

04:12.380 --> 04:16.580
ignored them. He wrongly assumed that the
numbers he could track told the entire

04:16.580 --> 04:22.940
story. Section 4. Two Tables, Two Worlds.
Managing Innovation. The tension at

04:22.940 --> 04:28.060
Danging Liu finally hit a breaking point
over a young chef named Ai Yuan. Ai Yuan

04:28.060 --> 04:32.240
wanted to invent something new, a green
peppercorn fish dish that relied on a

04:32.240 --> 04:36.600
really fresh, numbing flavor instead of
heavy oil. But, you know, innovation takes

04:36.600 --> 04:40.580
time. He had to experiment. Sometimes the
fish broke apart in the wok, sometimes the

04:40.580 --> 04:43.960
broth was way too thin. Because of all
this trial and error, his dish

04:43.960 --> 04:48.780
consistently failed Zhou Ming's strict KPI
table. So, Zhou Ming wanted to kill the

04:48.780 --> 04:53.060
project completely because it was dragging
down the kitchen's efficiency numbers. But

04:53.060 --> 04:57.260
here's where Aunt Lin stepped in. She
realized you simply cannot manage the

04:57.260 --> 05:01.820
unknown and the unknown in the exact same
way. So, she came up with a brilliant fix.

05:02.220 --> 05:06.620
Two separate systems. She kept Zhou Ming's
original KPI system, but designated it

05:06.620 --> 05:11.140
strictly as a diagnostic control.
Diagnostic controls are fantastic for

05:11.140 --> 05:14.840
mature, proven dishes, like the
traditional boiled beef. For those, you

05:14.840 --> 05:19.120
absolutely want consistency, you want
speed, and you want efficiency. But then,

05:19.240 --> 05:23.920
she hung up a second table right next to
the first one, exclusively for new dishes.

05:24.200 --> 05:28.840
This is an interactive control system.
Interactive controls are built for high

05:28.840 --> 05:33.020
-uncertainty environments. They aren't
obsessing over immediate profits or super

05:33.020 --> 05:37.140
-rigid speed targets. They're all about
continuous learning, testing hypotheses,

05:37.460 --> 05:41.880
and using stage-gated funding so that
innovation actually has room to breathe.

05:42.120 --> 05:47.000
So, this second table completely ignored
speed and margins. Instead, for A1's

05:47.000 --> 05:51.460
experimental fish dish, it just asked
three really vital questions. What did we

05:51.460 --> 05:56.540
verify today? What did we rule out? And
what changes next? That's it. This

05:56.540 --> 06:00.800
framework let A1 test his dish, fail
safely, and gather really valuable

06:00.800 --> 06:04.520
learnings without destroying the
restaurant's operational metrics. It

06:04.520 --> 06:07.880
proved that control doesn't have to kill
innovation. You just have to use the right

06:07.880 --> 06:13.900
kind of control. Section 5. Guarding your
core purpose. The minimum gates. The final

06:13.900 --> 06:17.940
lesson from Ding Ying Liu perfectly aligns
with academic research by Quinn and the

06:17.940 --> 06:22.440
Core on purpose-driven organizations. The
ultimate test of the restaurant's true

06:22.440 --> 06:27.820
purpose came when created a flaming spicy
chicken dish. This thing was an absolute

06:27.820 --> 06:32.100
monster hit with tourists. Waiters would
literally light it on fire right at the

06:32.100 --> 06:37.080
table. It looked amazing on social media.
It had massive profit margins and flawless

06:37.080 --> 06:42.880
KPI scores. Zero. That is exactly how much
space Aunt Lynn gave this dish on her

06:42.880 --> 06:48.120
menu. None at all. Why? Because despite
passing every single financial metric with

06:48.120 --> 06:52.980
flying colors, the dish was all internet
spectacle and absolutely zero authentic

06:52.980 --> 06:57.340
flavor. It sacrificed the proper
traditional cooking method just for a

06:57.340 --> 07:01.860
flashy presentation. To stop this from
happening again, Aunt Lynn set up three

07:01.860 --> 07:05.940
non-negotiable minimum gates for the
restaurant. Number one, ingredients

07:05.940 --> 07:11.220
absolutely cannot be unfresh. Two, heat
cannot be sacrificed for speed. And three,

07:11.440 --> 07:15.800
authentic roots cannot be lost just to
please everybody. She notoriously said she

07:15.800 --> 07:19.700
wanted to give people away from home a
true taste of their roots. If any project

07:19.700 --> 07:23.040
or dish violated these core rules, it was
dead in the water, no matter how

07:23.040 --> 07:27.400
profitable the KPI spreadsheet claimed it
would be. And honestly, this right here is

07:27.400 --> 07:32.680
what it really means to be purpose-driven.
It's not about slapping a nice, focus

07:32.680 --> 07:37.620
group-tested slogan on your lobby wall.
It's about making meaningful, shared goals

07:37.620 --> 07:42.040
genuinely dictate where you put your
resources. It means allowing accountable

07:42.040 --> 07:46.860
management judgment, and your core values,
to step up and override a high-ranking

07:46.860 --> 07:51.900
metric when that metric threatens the very
soul of your organization. So as we wrap

07:51.900 --> 07:54.880
up this explainer, I want you to take a
hard look at the spreadsheets and the

07:54.880 --> 07:58.780
dashboards that are driving behavior in
your own workplace right now. Are you

07:58.780 --> 08:02.080
using diagnostic controls in places where
you really should be using interactive

08:02.080 --> 08:06.960
ones? Are your metrics creating a sense of
false precision? And most importantly, I

08:06.960 --> 08:10.660
want to leave you with this final thought.
What is your organization's third spoon of

08:10.660 --> 08:14.840
red oil, that unquantifiable soul of your
product? And are your current metrics

08:14.840 --> 08:18.360
accidentally destroying it? Thank you so
much for joining this explainer. And

08:18.360 --> 08:20.480
remember, always keep questioning the
numbers.

08:27.400 --> 08:28.100
Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank
you. Thank you.
